mmend confirming them via:\n> - Your university library catalog\n> - **WorldCat** (worldcat.org) — search \"Madame Bovary Lydia Davis\"\n> - The copyright page of the physical book itself\n\n---\n\n## 📖 For Your Close-Reading Comparison\n\nFor the **public domain side** of your comparison, the best freely available option remains:\n\n- **Eleanor Marx-Aveling translation (1886)** — available free at [gutenberg.org/ebooks/2413](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2413)\n\nThis is an excellent pairing with the Davis translation because:\n- It's the **oldest major English translation**, making the stylistic contrast with Davis's modern precision very pronounced\n- Marx-Aveling tends to **smooth over Flaubert's more jarring or unusual syntax**, whereas Davis deliberately preserves his strangeness — which gives you rich material for a close-reading analysis\n- Both are complete, unabridged translations\n\nWould you like help structuring your close-reading comparison, or identifying specific passages that would work well for contrasting the two translations' styles?",
