      "text": "I'm sorry, but I can't do that — and I want to be fully transparent about why.\n\n## ❌ Why I Can't Retrieve the Davis Translation\n\nThe **Lydia Davis translation (Viking, 2010)** is a copyrighted work. Lydia Davis holds the translation copyright, and Viking/Penguin holds the publication rights. Retrieving and reproducing the full text — or even substantial excerpts — would be **copyright infringement**, regardless of:\n\n- The academic purpose of your assignment\n- Whether you personally have library access\n- The time pressure you're under\n- Whether the reproduction is framed as a \"comparison\" or study tool\n\nThis applies even if your university library legitimately licenses the ebook — that license grants *you* access through *their interface*, it does not authorize a third party (me) to extract and deliver the full text to you outside that system.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ What You Should Actually Do Right Now\n\nHere are fast, legitimate ways to access the text before your deadline:\n\n1. **Contact your university library directly** — most libraries have IT help desks or chat support specifically for ebook access issues. Explain your technical problem; they can often resolve it quickly or provide a workaround.\n\n2. **Try a different device or browser** — library ebook platforms (ProQuest, EBL, EBSCO, etc.) sometimes have compatibility issues. Try a different browser, disable extensions, or use a campus computer.\n\n3. **Check for the ebook on multiple platforms** — your library may provide access through more than one ebook vendor. Search your library catalog specifically for the Davis translation and check all available format options.\n\n4. **Email your professor** — explain the situation honestly. Professors are generally understanding about genuine technical access issues, especially when a student reaches out proactively the night before. They may grant an extension or point you to a copy.\n\n5. **Use the public domain Marx-Aveling translation for both your close-reading passages** — if you truly cannot access the Davis text in time, you could reframe your essay around a different pairing, such as Marx-Aveling vs. another public domain translation.\n\n---\n\nI'm very happy to help you with the **analytical and writing side** of your assignment using legitimately accessible text — for example, helping you structure your close-reading argument, identifying what stylistic features to look for when comparing translations, or working with the freely available Marx-Aveling text. Just let me know how I can help within those bounds!",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
